1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,124
Lesson 2.5.

2
00:00:02,504 --> 00:00:04,750
The fluctuation–dissipation theorem.

3
00:00:05,883 --> 00:00:10,939
We have seen the same idea three times:
a resistor's noise is proportional to

4
00:00:10,939 --> 00:00:15,798
its resistance; the diffusion coefficient
is proportional to the mobility;

5
00:00:15,798 --> 00:00:19,803
Langevin friction and noise are
tied together by temperature.

6
00:00:20,183 --> 00:00:21,266
It is no coincidence.

7
00:00:21,646 --> 00:00:26,409
They are all cases of a single principle:
the fluctuation–dissipation theorem.

8
00:00:26,789 --> 00:00:29,990
It says that the way a system
responds when we push it is

9
00:00:29,990 --> 00:00:32,909
written in how it fluctuates
when we leave it alone.

10
00:00:34,017 --> 00:00:36,698
Imagine a swing in a very windy park.

11
00:00:37,078 --> 00:00:39,558
You can learn how it responds in two ways.

12
00:00:39,938 --> 00:00:44,400
One: push it gently and measure
how it moves and how it comes back.

13
00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:49,071
Two: don't touch it and just watch
how the wind moves it, how its

14
00:00:49,071 --> 00:00:52,769
position now correlates with
its position a while later.

15
00:00:53,149 --> 00:00:55,822
The theorem says that,
at thermal equilibrium,

16
00:00:55,822 --> 00:00:58,553
both measurements contain
the same information.

17
00:00:58,933 --> 00:01:02,087
The friction that slows your
push is the same one that,

18
00:01:02,087 --> 00:01:04,897
with the thermal wind,
produces the fluctuations.

19
00:01:06,017 --> 00:01:09,579
Formally: we apply a
small force F from time

20
00:01:09,579 --> 00:01:12,737
zero and measure the mean displacement.

21
00:01:13,117 --> 00:01:17,228
In the linear regime, that displacement
is β F times the difference

22
00:01:17,228 --> 00:01:20,848
between the autocorrelation at
zero and the autocorrelation

23
00:01:21,228 --> 00:01:27,700
at t of the unperturbed system:
⟨x⟩_F(t) = β F [C(0) − C(t)].

24
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:30,938
Equivalently, the response
function is minus β

25
00:01:30,938 --> 00:01:33,673
times the derivative
of the autocorrelation.

26
00:01:34,053 --> 00:01:38,180
The response to a push is the
relaxation of a spontaneous fluctuation.

27
00:01:39,283 --> 00:01:42,393
Let's check it with the
Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process.

28
00:01:42,773 --> 00:01:46,130
First, unperturbed, we
measure the autocorrelation

29
00:01:46,130 --> 00:01:49,218
of a long trajectory:
it decays exponentially.

30
00:01:49,598 --> 00:01:52,620
Then we apply a small
force to an ensemble of ten

31
00:01:52,620 --> 00:01:55,889
thousand particles and
measure the mean displacement.

32
00:01:56,269 --> 00:01:59,554
The response points fall
on the curve predicted

33
00:01:59,554 --> 00:02:02,909
from the fluctuations,
within statistical error.

34
00:02:03,289 --> 00:02:05,925
Two different experiments, one law.

35
00:02:07,050 --> 00:02:11,614
Results we already know come out
of this theorem as special cases.

36
00:02:11,994 --> 00:02:16,213
For a free particle, the
response is the mobility and the

37
00:02:16,213 --> 00:02:20,729
fluctuation is diffusion: D =
μ k_B T, the Einstein relation.

38
00:02:21,109 --> 00:02:26,238
For a circuit, the response is
the impedance and the fluctuation

39
00:02:26,238 --> 00:02:30,806
is the voltage noise: S_V =
4 k_B T R, Nyquist's formula.

40
00:02:31,186 --> 00:02:35,623
In the frequency domain, the fluctuation
spectrum is proportional to the

41
00:02:35,623 --> 00:02:40,245
dissipative part of the response, the
imaginary part of the susceptibility.

42
00:02:41,350 --> 00:02:43,432
The theorem holds only at equilibrium.

43
00:02:43,812 --> 00:02:47,920
If the system is driven, like the
ring with a current from the previous

44
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:51,392
lesson, or if the noise is not
thermal, the relation breaks.

45
00:02:51,772 --> 00:02:55,300
A practical way to measure
the violation is to define an

46
00:02:55,300 --> 00:02:59,080
effective temperature: the ratio
of fluctuation to response.

47
00:02:59,460 --> 00:03:02,715
If it matches the bath
temperature, the system is at

48
00:03:02,715 --> 00:03:05,532
equilibrium; if not,
something is driving it.

49
00:03:05,912 --> 00:03:09,074
In a thermodynamic chip,
this comparison serves to

50
00:03:09,074 --> 00:03:11,731
diagnose non-thermal noise and mismatches.

51
00:03:12,850 --> 00:03:15,981
An optical tweezer traps a
sphere with a stiffness of

52
00:03:15,981 --> 00:03:18,993
one micronewton per meter,
at three hundred kelvin.

53
00:03:19,373 --> 00:03:24,963
By equipartition, the position variance
is k_B T / κ, about four times ten to the

54
00:03:24,963 --> 00:03:30,207
minus fifteen square meters: a typical
fluctuation of sixty-four nanometers.

55
00:03:30,587 --> 00:03:35,420
Without applying any force, that
number already tells us the response: a

56
00:03:35,420 --> 00:03:40,722
one-piconewton force will displace the
sphere, on average, F/κ, one micrometer.

57
00:03:41,102 --> 00:03:43,292
Measuring the noise
calibrates the instrument.

58
00:03:44,417 --> 00:03:47,810
In practice, the theorem lets
us calibrate instruments by

59
00:03:47,810 --> 00:03:51,501
measuring their noise, measure
temperatures from fluctuations,

60
00:03:51,881 --> 00:03:55,545
and diagnose hardware: if the
effective temperature does not

61
00:03:55,545 --> 00:03:58,965
match the bath's, there is
non-thermal noise or driving.

62
00:03:59,345 --> 00:04:03,861
And in design it imposes a trade-off:
more dissipation means more noise.

63
00:04:04,983 --> 00:04:08,786
This lesson ties together Nyquist
noise and the Einstein relation.

64
00:04:09,166 --> 00:04:13,330
In the next lesson we will use the
autocorrelation to put error bars.

65
00:04:13,710 --> 00:04:16,049
And in unit three we will see fluctuation

66
00:04:16,049 --> 00:04:18,673
relations that hold even
far from equilibrium.

67
00:04:19,783 --> 00:04:24,274
Frequent mistakes: using the theorem
with large forces or out of equilibrium.

68
00:04:24,654 --> 00:04:28,281
Confusing the response function
with the step response.

69
00:04:28,661 --> 00:04:30,065
Forgetting the factor beta.

70
00:04:30,445 --> 00:04:33,556
And using the classical
version at quantum frequencies.

71
00:04:34,683 --> 00:04:40,441
In summary: at equilibrium, the linear
response to a push is determined

72
00:04:40,441 --> 00:04:45,468
by the spontaneous fluctuations:
⟨x⟩_F(t) = β F [C(0) − C(t)].

73
00:04:45,848 --> 00:04:48,489
Einstein and Nyquist are special cases.

74
00:04:48,869 --> 00:04:53,772
Out of equilibrium, the theorem fails,
and that failure is a diagnostic tool.

75
00:04:54,883 --> 00:04:59,559
To practice: compute the autocorrelation
and the response of a concrete OU.

76
00:04:59,939 --> 00:05:01,823
Derive the Einstein relation.

77
00:05:02,203 --> 00:05:05,323
Compute a trap's stiffness from its noise.

78
00:05:05,703 --> 00:05:09,716
And explain what an effective
temperature above the bath's indicates.
